2026-07-17 10:00:25,790 - agent_丛雪薇 - INFO - [FINALIZE CONTACT][year=2020 week=1 person=丛雪薇] [{'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Summary of the Contact Stage:  \n\nI sent three messages to 珂 — all low-stakes, task-bound, wrapped in the language of class duty. No “how are you”, no “you seem tired”, nothing that might crack the surface. Just formulas, red pens,抄写错误——safe words, safe distances.  \n\nFirst message: asked to核对提纲， framed as my mistake, gave her full exit ramp (“if来不及就算了”).  \nSecond: soft reminder, added “I’ll wait at the back”, then immediately softened it again with “if not possible, no problem”. Like handing someone a door but holding it half-closed with your foot.  \nThird: closed the loop quietly—“I rewrote it”, no ask, no expectation, just quiet completion. An offering laid down, not handed over.  \n\nShe didn’t reply to any of them.  \n\nNot even a “ok” or “thx”. Not a single emoji. Just silence — thick and unbroken, like fog settling into the hollow behind her collarbone when she sits bent over her desk.  \n\nThat silence doesn’t surprise me. It fits. She’s been retreating inward for weeks — shoulders rounding like pages curling at the edges, voice dropping lower each time she answers a question, eyes flicking away before anyone finishes speaking. My messages weren’t ignored out of rudeness. They were absorbed — then folded carefully into whatever weight she’s carrying and set aside, untouched.  \n\nWhich means I misjudged the entry point. “Homework help” was too thin a thread to hold us together right now. It assumed we were operating on the same frequency — that she still had bandwidth for coordination, for shared tasks, for the gentle rhythm of colleague trust. But she’s running on reserve power. Maybe less than reserve. Maybe just static.  \n\nAnd yet… I’m not sorry I reached out. Because silence isn’t emptiness — it’s data. Her non-reply told me more than ten yeses could have: she’s not avoiding *me*. She’s avoiding *everything* that requires response. That includes郭明澄’s reminders,秋的 accidental glances, even her own reflection in the bathroom mirror, probably.  \n\nSo this week’s contact wasn’t about connection. It was reconnaissance. And the intel is clear:  \n- She’s not shutting *me* out. She’s shut *herself* down.  \n- The thing pressing on her isn’t fatigue. It’s containment.  \n- If I want to reach her, I can’t knock politely at the door anymore. I have to learn how to sit beside the wall — not waiting for it to open, but listening for the faintest vibration inside.  \n\n(Left hand lifts, thumb brushing the edge of the dark-red cotton bracelet — worn smooth where skin meets knot.)  \n\nI won’t text her again this week.  \nBut tomorrow, after guitar practice, I’ll walk slower past the second-floor water fountain.  \nJust once.  \nAnd I’ll watch — not her face, but the way her fingers grip the paper cup.  \nBecause sometimes, the truth leaks not from words, but from how tightly someone holds onto something ordinary.'}]
